    "knowledge_point": "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically",
    "problem": "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \n$$",
    "worked_solution": "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/assets/47615688bf550384de29a5e7e480bdab699b8eee994e5e28052e4d043e2db503.png)",
